---
// English version of G3 (src/guides/IncheonReform.astro, slug incheon-reform-2026). Data and counting code are the same as the Korean file.
// Laws and dates: National Law Information Center text, Incheon and Ministry of the Interior and Safety press releases.
// Old dong -> new gu table, areas and the before-reform map come from the pipeline (src/data/incheon-reform.json, src/data/svg/incheon-before.json)
import RegionMap from '../components/RegionMap.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/paths';
import { region, svgOf, countKinds, enNameOf, enDongs, enShortOf } from '../lib/geo';
import reform from '../data/incheon-reform.json';

const r = region('incheon');
const svg = svgOf('incheon');
const idOf = (ko: string) => r.items.find((it) => it.ko === ko)!.id;
const NEW = reform.reform.map((x) => x.new);
const all = svg.items.flatMap((it) => it.dongs);
const k = countKinds(all);
const total = (row: (typeof reform.reform)[number]) => Object.values(row.from).flat().length;
// Areas are the figures Incheon publishes (administrative reorganization page, updated June 17, 2026). Areas measured from the boundary data (row.area_km2) are larger, probably because they include sea, tidal flats and unregistered reclaimed land, but the cause is unconfirmed (Geomdan-gu +12.7 km²)
const OFFICIAL_AREA: Record<string, number> = { 제물포구: 21.8, 영종구: 125.88, 서해구: 71.36, 검단구: 47.7 };
const areaOf = (row: (typeof reform.reform)[number]) => OFFICIAL_AREA[row.new] ?? row.area_km2;
const boundaryOf = (name: string) => reform.reform.find((x) => x.new === name)!.area_km2;
const biggest = [...reform.reform].sort((a, b) => areaOf(b) - areaOf(a))[0];
const lessons = r.lessons.filter((l) => l.items.some((id) => NEW.map(idOf).includes(id)));
const nm = (id: string) => enNameOf(id);
const newEn = (ko: string) => enNameOf(idOf(ko));
// old districts (before July 2026), keyed by the Korean name used in the data
const oldEn = (ko: string) => enShortOf(`old:${ko}`);
// Korean neighborhood name -> English name (same order as the boundary data)
const DONG_EN = new Map<string, string>();
svg.items.forEach((it) => { const en = enDongs(it.id); it.dongs.forEach((d, i) => { if (en[i]) DONG_EN.set(d, en[i]); }); });
const dongEn = (ko: string) => DONG_EN.get(ko) ?? ko;
const SRC = {
  law: 'https://www.law.go.kr/법령/인천광역시제물포구ㆍ영종구및검단구설치등에관한법률',
  city: 'https://www.incheon.go.kr/IC01070105/3012613',
  rename: 'https://www.mois.go.kr/frt/bbs/type010/commonSelectBoardArticle.do?bbsId=BBSMSTR_000000000008&nttId=125833',
};
---
<div class="summary">
  <b>Key points</b>
  <span>1. On July 1, 2026, Incheon's Jung-gu and Dong-gu became Jemulpo-gu and Yeongjong-gu, and Seo-gu became Seohae-gu and Geomdan-gu.</span>
  <span>2. Incheon went from 2 counties and 8 districts to 2 counties and 9 districts. The Yeongjong Island area is Yeongjong-gu. The old Dong-gu and the rest of the old Jung-gu form Jemulpo-gu.</span>
  <span>3. The tables below show which new district each old neighborhood (dong) moved to. You can look up a neighborhood by its name.</span>
</div>

<h2>Before and after</h2>
<p>The Act on the Establishment of Jemulpo-gu, Yeongjong-gu and Geomdan-gu, Incheon Metropolitan City, called "the Act" below, abolishes Jung-gu and Dong-gu, sets up Jemulpo-gu, Yeongjong-gu and Geomdan-gu, and separates the area that becomes Geomdan-gu from Seo-gu. Article 1 says its purpose is to make life more convenient for residents and to promote balanced regional development. On the same day, Seo-gu changed its name to Seohae-gu. So three old districts (Jung-gu, Dong-gu, Seo-gu) changed, and there are now four districts (Jemulpo-gu, Yeongjong-gu, Seohae-gu, Geomdan-gu).</p>
<figure class="pair">
  <div>
    <RegionMap en region="incheon-before" labels alwaysLabel={['old:동구']} highlight={['old:중구', 'old:동구', 'old:서구']} fit={['old:중구', 'old:동구', 'old:서구']} label="Map of Incheon before the reorganization, with 2 counties and 8 districts: the old Jung-gu, Dong-gu and Seo-gu, which changed, are shaded yellow" />
    <p class="meta">Before the reorganization (until June 2026) · 2 counties, 8 districts</p>
  </div>
  <div>
    <RegionMap en region="incheon" labels alwaysLabel={[idOf('제물포구')]} highlight={NEW.map(idOf)} fit={NEW.map(idOf)} label="Map of Incheon after the reorganization, with 2 counties and 9 districts: the new or renamed Jemulpo-gu, Yeongjong-gu, Seohae-gu and Geomdan-gu are shaded yellow" />
    <p class="meta">After the reorganization (from July 2026) · 2 counties, 9 districts</p>
  </div>
</figure>
<p class="meta">Both maps are drawn with the October 1, 2026 administrative neighborhood boundaries. The map before the reorganization groups today's neighborhoods by their old district, so the coastline is the same on both maps.</p>

<h2>From old districts to new districts</h2>
<p>When we match the neighborhood boundaries before the reorganization (April 1, 2026 version) and after it (October 1, 2026 version) by neighborhood name, every old neighborhood moved into exactly one new district. No neighborhood was split in two or lies across two districts. By area, {newEn(biggest.new)} is the largest.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table class="nums">
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Current district</th><th scope="col">Old district</th><th scope="col" class="num">Neighborhoods</th><th scope="col" class="num">Area (approx., km²)</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>{reform.reform.map((row) => (
    <tr><td>{newEn(row.new)}</td><td>{Object.entries(row.from).map(([o, list]) => `${oldEn(o)} ${(list as string[]).length}`).join(' · ')}</td><td class="num">{total(row)}</td><td class="num">{areaOf(row)}</td></tr>
  ))}</tbody>
</table></div>
<p class="meta">The areas are the figures published on Incheon's website. They seem to use the same basis as the registered land areas in the city's cadastral statistics (the city's figures for the old Jung-gu, Dong-gu and Seo-gu add up to those totals). If this site measures from the neighborhood boundary data instead, all four districts come out larger, and Geomdan-gu especially so, at {boundaryOf('검단구')} km², which is 12.7 km² more than the city's figure. This is probably because the boundaries also include sea, tidal flats and reclaimed land that is not yet registered, but we could not confirm the exact cause. Most of that gap comes from Oryuwanggil-dong.</p>
<h3>Find a neighborhood by name</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Current district</th><th scope="col">Neighborhoods that came from the old districts</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>{reform.reform.map((row) => (
    <tr><td>{newEn(row.new)}</td><td>{Object.entries(row.from).map(([o, list]) => <p class="from"><b>Old {oldEn(o)}</b> {(list as string[]).map(dongEn).join(' · ')}</p>)}</td></tr>
  ))}</tbody>
</table></div>

<h2>When, and under which law</h2>
<div class="tbl"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Date</th><th scope="col">Event</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>January 30, 2024</td><td>The Act is promulgated (Act No. 20161). It takes effect on July 1, 2026</td></tr>
    <tr><td>September 12, 2024</td><td>Incheon starts a research project to design the organization and staffing of the new districts</td></tr>
    <tr><td>December 30, 2025</td><td>The Act is amended (Act No. 21247): it adds special provisions, such as support from the state and Incheon for the cost of setting up the new districts</td></tr>
    <tr><td>May 2026</td><td>The National Assembly passes the Bill on Changing the Name of Seo-gu, Incheon Metropolitan City: Seo-gu becomes Seohae-gu</td></tr>
    <tr><td>July 1, 2026</td><td>The Act takes effect. Jemulpo-gu, Yeongjong-gu and Geomdan-gu are set up, and Seo-gu becomes Seohae-gu</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The addenda to the Act say that the heads and council members of the new districts are elected on the local election day before July 1, and that Jung-gu and Dong-gu, which are being abolished, hold no election for district head. Incheon described the reorganization as growing the city's administrative system from 2 counties and 8 districts to 2 counties and 9 districts for the first time since 1995, after 31 years.</p>

<h2>Incheon today</h2>
<p>Incheon Metropolitan City is made up of Ganghwa-gun, Ongjin-gun and 9 districts. It has {k.읍} eup (towns), {k.면} myeon (townships) and {k.동} dong (neighborhoods), {all.length} in total (October 1, 2026 version). The four changed districts consist only of dong. The eup and myeon are in Ganghwa-gun and Ongjin-gun.</p>

<h2>Tips for learning them on the map</h2>
<ul>
  <li><b>Yeongjong-gu</b> is Yeongjong Island, where Incheon International Airport is, and the Yongyu area to its west. It is easy to remember as one island that is one district.</li>
  <li><b>Jemulpo-gu</b> is the area around Incheon Station and Dongincheon Station. It combines the whole old Dong-gu with the part of the old Jung-gu that is not on Yeongjong Island.</li>
  <li><b>Geomdan-gu</b> is the northern end of the old Seo-gu, next to Gimpo. <b>Seohae-gu</b> is the area to its south, around Cheongna, Gajeong and Seongnam-dong.</li>
  <li>On Fill the Map, you meet the new districts in {lessons.map((l) => `lesson ${l.no} (${l.items.filter((id) => NEW.map(idOf).includes(id)).map(nm).join(', ')})`).join(', ')}.</li>
</ul>
<div class="cta"><b>Try it on the map</b><a class="btn" href={href(`/en/#/kr/incheon/sgg/p${lessons[0].no}`)}>Start Incheon lesson {lessons[0].no}</a></div>

<h2>Sources</h2>
<ul class="src">
  <li>National Law Information Center, <a href={SRC.law}>Act on the Establishment of Jemulpo-gu, Yeongjong-gu and Geomdan-gu, Incheon Metropolitan City</a> (enacted as Act No. 20161 on January 30, 2024; amended as Act No. 21247 on December 30, 2025; effective July 1, 2026), Articles 1 and 2 and the addenda</li>
  <li>Incheon Metropolitan City, <a href={SRC.city}>Incheon designs the organization and staffing of the four districts launching in 2026</a> (press release, September 12, 2024)</li>
  <li>Ministry of the Interior and Safety, <a href={SRC.rename}>Incheon's Seo-gu makes a fresh start as Seohae-gu, bringing residents' wishes together</a> (May 7, 2026)</li>
  <li>Incheon Metropolitan City, <a href="https://www.incheon.go.kr/IC01070101">Incheon website page on the administrative reorganization</a> (areas of the new districts, updated June 17, 2026)</li>
  <li>Neighborhood boundaries: as of April 1, 2026 (before the reorganization) and October 1, 2026 (after the reorganization). The correspondence table and the map before the reorganization were made from these boundaries.</li>
</ul>


<style>
  /* before/after map pair: side by side inside the 760px article box, stacked when narrow. Spacing of neighborhood lists comes from the shared .tbl */
  .pair { margin: 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr)); gap: var(--sp-5); }
  .pair > div { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--sp-2); min-width: 0; }
</style>
